---
title: Server transforms
summary: Render custom elements on the server, or rewrite the whole page, even inside islands. `rewrite()` from `@ogygia/rewrite` is a Kit handle; before anything hydrates, ogygia puts back whatever your render moved in Svelte's markup.
related:
  - regions/server-islands
  - regions/lakes
  - reference/constraints
---

Some apps run one more pass over the HTML after rendering: a server render for web components, a translation proxy, a tool that edits markup. Islands make this hard, because an island hydrates against the exact markup Svelte rendered. A pass that moves a node, trims a space or wraps a child breaks the hydration walk, and the island throws its server DOM away.

`@ogygia/rewrite` lets that pass run anyway. ogygia marks the HTML before your render, reads what it did after, and in the browser puts back exactly what hydration needs before anything hydrates. Apps without such a pass don't install it and ship none of this.

```bash
pnpm add @ogygia/rewrite
```

```ts
// vite.config.ts
import { ogygia } from 'ogygia/vite';
import { rewrite } from '@ogygia/rewrite/vite';

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [ogygia(), rewrite(), sveltekit()]
});
```

`rewrite()` in vite.config takes no options. Leave it out and every request fails with `rewrite_plugin_missing`.

## Rendering custom elements

```ts
// src/lib/server/components.ts
import { rewrite } from '@ogygia/rewrite';
import { renderBatch } from '$lib/server/renderer';

export const components = rewrite({
  match: 'my-',
  async render(elements, ctx) {
    const rendered = await renderBatch(elements.map((el) => el.html), { signal: ctx.signal });
    return rendered.map((r) => ({ html: r.html, sheets: r.sheets }));
  }
});
```

```ts
// src/hooks.server.ts
import { sequence } from '@sveltejs/kit/hooks';
import * as ogygia from 'ogygia/server';
import { components } from '$lib/server/components';

export const handle = sequence(components, ogygia.handle());
```

- **`match`** picks the elements: a tag prefix (`'my-'`), a list of tags (`['my-card', 'my-nav']`), or a test on the name. Only the outermost match is sent. A match inside it goes with it.
- **`render(elements, ctx)`** is called once per chunk of the page, with every match in that chunk, and once per region answer. It returns one result per element, in order: `{ html, sheets }`, or just the HTML string.
- **`ctx`** has `kind` (`'document'` or `'region'`), `csr` (Kit hydrates the page), `url`, `event`, and `signal`, which fires when the request is cancelled.
- A chunk with no match goes out byte for byte, and `render` is not called.

Each element's `html` has ogygia's marks numbered from 0, so the same element has the same bytes on every page. A cache can key on it. Equal elements in one call are sent once.

### The page streams

A streamed page goes chunk by chunk. Each chunk's matches are rendered in one call and sent with that chunk. An element that starts in one chunk and ends in a later one (an `{#await}` inside it) goes out unrendered, and dev says `rewrite_element_split`.

### When a render fails

- If the batch throws or returns the wrong number of results, each element is tried again on its own.
- An element that still fails goes out as ogygia rendered it and renders in the browser. Only that element is affected. ogygia warns `rewrite_element_failed` once per response and element name, in every build.
- A result that drops `og-h` from an element ogygia restores goes out the same way, with `rewrite_marks_lost`.
- A result whose HTML does not close what it opens fails too: it would take in the rest of the page.
- When the request was cancelled, nothing is retried and nothing is said.

## Rendering an element ogygia restores

An element `render` gets with `restored: true` carries `og-h`. ogygia puts it back for Svelte before anything hydrates, so render it in "scoped" form with three additions:

```html
<my-button og-h="0" og-shadow="my-button.shadow" og-keep="icon-name" icon-name="star" class="sc-my-button-h">
  <span class="btn sc-my-button">
    <slot class="sc-my-button sc-my-button-s"><!--og-c 0.0 t-->Sign in</slot>
  </span>
</my-button>
```

- **`<slot>` wrappers.** Where the component would slot the host's children, wrap them in a real `<slot>` element. Outside a shadow root a `<slot>` renders as if it were not there, so the page paints exactly like your scoped render.
- **`og-shadow="key …"`** names the shadow sheets for the host's root (see [Sheets](#sheets)).
- **`og-keep="attr …"`** (optional) lists attributes you added that must survive the restore. A kept attribute must stay true of the live element, since a hole answer copies attributes onto live elements.

An `og-h` element without `og-shadow` is left exactly as Svelte wrote it. Every other `og-*` mark is ogygia's: carry it along wherever you move a node. Don't put a marker of your own between an ogygia mark comment and its node.

An element with `restored: false` is yours: render it any way you like.

ogygia restores an element when it sits in markup Svelte owns (an island, or the whole body of a [Kit-hydrated page](/docs/regions/client-islands)), when Svelte-owned markup or a hole sits inside it, and always in a region answer.

## Sheets

A result's `sheets` are `{ key, css, scope }`:

- `scope: 'page'` becomes a `<style>` for the document;
- `scope: 'shadow'` is a sheet a restored element names in `og-shadow`, built once and adopted by every root that names its key.

Each key goes out once per response. While the head is still open, a sheet goes into `<head>`. Once it has gone out, a sheet goes inline right before the first element that needs it. In a region answer it goes inline, and the runtime moves it into the head.

## What ogygia restores, and when

For each restored element, innermost first and in one task:

1. an open shadow root, with its sheets;
2. the rendered tree moved into it, so its `<slot>` wrappers become real slots;
3. Svelte's children back in the light DOM, exactly as Svelte rendered them;
4. the element's attributes back to Svelte's, except those in `og-keep`.

It runs before anything reads the markup: inline, right after the chunk that has the element (the first one carries the restorer, a later one a short call); on a region answer before it goes in; on an incoming page before a router swap. A page where nothing was restored ships no restorer.

A tag the HTML parser would restructure where your render made it (an `<a>` in the page's `<a>`, a `<div>` in a `<p>`) ships as a stand-in and comes back as the real element.

## Several rewrites

```ts
export const handle = sequence(faq, components, ogygia.handle());
```

They run in the order listed, on one marking of the HTML: a later one sees an earlier one's output, marks intact. A rewrite listed after `ogygia.handle()` fails with `rewrite_after_ogygia`.

## The whole page

```ts
export const pdf = rewrite({
  page: async (html, ctx) => toPrintable(html)
});
```

`page` gets the whole document or region answer. A document it runs on is held until its last chunk. A `page` function that throws, or drops ogygia's marks, sends the HTML as ogygia rendered it and says `rewrite_page_failed` (dev throws it). `pdf.html(str)` runs the function on a plain string, outside any request.

## Testing a rewrite

```ts
import { components } from '$lib/server/components';

it('restores the light DOM exactly', async () => {
  const r = await components.check(markup, { kind: 'region' });
  expect(r.failed).toEqual([]);
  expect(r.mismatches).toEqual([]);
  expect(r.restored).toBeGreaterThan(0);
});
```

`.check(html, { kind, csr })` runs the whole round trip: mark, your render, settle, then (with a DOM, as in vitest browser mode or jsdom) parse, restore, and compare each restored element with Svelte's original. `failed` lists each element whose render failed. Without a DOM, `dom` is `null` and `mismatches` is empty.

## The dev check

In dev, ogygia sends each restored element's original children with the page, and the restore compares them: node types, tags, text, comments and attributes. A difference is an error in the console naming the first node that differs, and an `ogygia:restore-mismatch` event:

```
[ogygia] the rewrite broke hydration markup: <my-button> > [1]: Svelte has <i>, the restored markup has nothing
```
